            <ArticleTitle>Epidemiology of hepatocellular carcinoma: consider the population.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S2-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MCG.0b013e3182872f29</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is increasing in incidence and has a very high fatality rate. Cirrhosis due to chronic hepatitis B or hepatitis C is the leading risk factor for HCC. Global epidemiology of HCC is determined by the prevalence of dominant viral hepatitis and the age it is acquired in the underlying population. Upcoming risk factors include obesity, diabetes, and related nonalcoholic fatty liver disease. This review discusses the latest trends of HCC globally and in the United States. It also provides an evidence-based commentary on the risk factors and lists some of the preventive measures to reduce the incidence of HCC.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
